a histogram?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the bars have different widths, so height is density not frequency; reading the heights suggests three times as many when it is only one and a half times"],"keywords":[["bars"],["different"],["widths"],["height"],["density"],["frequency"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State what the areas of the two bars represent and give their values.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Area = width × height.","Compare the areas with your frequencies."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the area of each bar is its frequency, so the areas are 30 and 20"],"keywords":[["area"],["frequency"],["areas"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the width 20, 1 for 20. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the widths, 1 for the 3 : 2 explanation. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''areas = frequencies'', 1 for both values.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the width 20, 1 for 20. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the widths, 1 for the 3 : 2 explanation. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''areas = frequencies'', 1 for both values.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the grouped table in the workspace; the chart will plot the densities for you to check.","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the width 20, 1 for 20. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the widths, 1 for the 3 : 2 explanation. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''areas = frequencies'', 1 for both values."},{"n":39,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A machine fills bags labelled ''500 g''. A sample of five bags weighs 496, 498, 500, 502 and 504 g.\n\n(a) Calculate the mean mass.\n(b) Calculate the deviations from the mean.\n(c) Calculate the population standard deviation.\n(d) A second sample gives a standard deviation of 6.1 g. Explain what this tells the quality manager, and which sample is closer to the label.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A machine fills bags labelled ''500 g''. A sample of five bags weighs 496, 498, 500, 502 and 504 g."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the mean mass.\n(b) Calculate the deviations from the mean.\n(c) Calculate the population standard deviation.\n(d) A second sample gives a standard deviation of 6.1 g. Explain what this tells the quality manager, and which sample is closer to the label."}]}]},"e":"Mean 500 g; Σ(x − x̄)² = 40; variance 8; σ ≈ 2.83 g. The second sample''s σ of 6.1 g shows far more variability in filling, so it is less precise, although its mean could still be 500 g.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"standard deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"σ measures precision (spread), while the mean measures accuracy (level). Report both when judging a process.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the mean mass, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Add the five masses and divide by 5.","The total is 2500 g."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":500,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate Σ(x − x̄)² for the sample.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Subtract 500 from each mass, then square.","16 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 16."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":40,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the population standard deviation, to 3 significant figures.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Variance = 40 ÷ 5.","Take the square root of 8."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":2.83,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"g"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain the meaning of σ = 6.1 g for the second sample.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What does a larger standard deviation measure?","Does the mean have to change as well?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["the second sample''s masses vary more about their mean, so the process is less precise; a standard deviation of 6.1 g is much larger than 2.83 g so that filling is less consistent","it shows greater variability in filling, so the second process is less precise even if its mean is on target"],"keywords":[[["standard deviation","spread","variation","variability","consisten"],["smaller","larger","closer","more","less","compare","mean"]],[["sample","small","few","20 spins","20 seeds","200 spins","variation","chance"],["too small","random","not enough","larger","more trials","expected","difference"]]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 500 g. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 40. (c) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by 5, 1 for the variance 8, 1 for 2.83. (d) 3 marks: 1 for ''more variable'', 1 for ''less precise'', 1 for separating accuracy from precision.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 500 g. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 40. (c) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by 5, 1 for the variance 8, 1 for 2.83. (d) 3 marks: 1 for ''more variable'', 1 for ''less precise'', 1 for separating accuracy from precision.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the five masses in the workspace as a dot plot to see the symmetry before computing.","statistics_chart":"dot_plot"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 500 g. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the deviations, 1 for 40. (c) 3 marks: 1 for dividing by 5, 1 for the variance 8, 1 for 2.83. (d) 3 marks: 1 for ''more variable'', 1 for ''less precise'', 1 for separating accuracy from precision."},{"n":40,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A cinema surveys 80 customers about travel time (minutes) to the cinema:\n\n0 ≤ t < 10: 20\n10 ≤ t < 20: 28\n20 ≤ t < 40: 24\n40 ≤ t < 60: 8\n\n(a) Calculate the estimated mean travel time.\n(b) Explain one limitation of the estimated mean compared with the true mean.\n(c) The cinema proposes a free bus for customers who travel more than 20 minutes. Using your table, state how many would qualify, and explain one assumption your answer relies on.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cinema surveys 80 customers about travel time (minutes) to the cinema:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 ≤ t < 10: 20\n10 ≤ t < 20: 28\n20 ≤ t < 40: 24\n40 ≤ t < 60: 8"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the estimated mean travel time.\n(b) Explain one limitation of the estimated mean compared with the true mean.\n(c) The cinema proposes a free bus for customers who travel more than 20 minutes. Using your table, state how many would qualify, and explain one assumption your answer relies on."}]}]},"e":"Σf x = 1640 over 80 customers gives 20.5 minutes. The midpoint assumption flattens variation inside the wide classes. Customers over 20 minutes are 24 + 8 = 32, assuming the class boundary matches ''more than 20''.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"grouped data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":40,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An estimated mean from grouped data is always an approximation; state the midpoint assumption when you interpret it.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate Σ(f × midpoint).","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Midpoints are 5, 15, 30 and 50.","Multiply each by its frequency and add."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1640,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the estimated mean travel time, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The estimated mean is Σ(f × midpoint) divided by the total frequency.","Divide your 1640 by the 80 customers."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":20.5,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain one limitation of this estimated mean.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Where does the estimate place every value in a class?","Is that realistic?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["it assumes every value in a class is at the midpoint, so it ignores the real spread within each class and is only an estimate"],"keywords":[["assumes"],["value"],["class"],["midpoint"],["ignores"],["real"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State how many customers travel more than 20 minutes, and one assumption behind it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Add the two classes above 20 minutes.","Does the table show anyone exactly at 20 minutes?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["32 customers, assuming the 20 ≤ t < 40 class is correctly classified as more than 20 minutes and that each customer answered once"],"keywords":[["customers"],["assuming"],["class"],["correctly"],["classified"],["minutes"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for 1640. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 80, 1 for 20.5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint assumption, 1 for its effect. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 32, 1 for a valid assumption.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for 1640. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 80, 1 for 20.5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint assumption, 1 for its effect. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 32, 1 for a valid assumption.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Draw the histogram in the workspace (the 20-minute and 20-wide classes need densities) and use it in (c).","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoints, 1 for 1640. (b) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 80, 1 for 20.5. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the midpoint assumption, 1 for its effect. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 32, 1 for a valid assumption."},{"n":41,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A school records the number of minutes 50 students spend on homework. The cumulative frequency curve shows the median at 42 minutes, Q1 at 28 minutes and Q3 at 58 minutes.\n\n(a) State the interquartile range.\n(b) Explain what the IQR tells the school that the median alone does not.\n(c) A teacher claims ''most students work about 42 minutes''. Evaluate the claim using the quartiles.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A school records the number of minutes 50 students spend on homework. The cumulative frequency curve shows the median at 42 minutes, Q1 at 28 minutes and Q3 at 58 minutes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the interquartile range.\n(b) Explain what the IQR tells the school that the median alone does not.\n(c) A teacher claims ''most students work about 42 minutes''. Evaluate the claim using the quartiles."}]}]},"e":"IQR = 30 minutes. The median locates the middle student; the IQR shows the middle half span 28–58 minutes, so the teacher''s ''about 42 minutes'' is a central value that conceals wide variation.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"cumulative frequency","st":"cumulative frequency","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":41,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Pairs of measures tell the whole story: a measure of centre plus a measure of spread.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the interquartile range, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["IQR = Q3 − Q1.","58 − 28."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":30,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain what the IQR adds to the median.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["What does the IQR measure?","Which half of the students does it describe?"],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["the IQR describes the spread of the middle half of the students, whereas the median gives only one central value"],"keywords":[["describes"],["spread"],["middle"],["half"],["students"],["whereas"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate the teacher''s claim using the quartiles.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Half the students work more than 42 minutes and half less.","How wide is the range of the middle half?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the median does show a typical student at 42 minutes, but the middle half spread over 28 to 58 minutes, so ''about 42 minutes'' hides a wide variation"],"keywords":[["median"],["show"],["typical"],["student"],["minutes"],["middle"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''spread of the middle half'', 1 for the contrast with the median. (c) 3 marks: 1 for accepting the median value, 1 for citing 28–58, 1 for judging the claim''s limitation.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''spread of the middle half'', 1 for the contrast with the median. (c) 3 marks: 1 for accepting the median value, 1 for citing 28–58, 1 for judging the claim''s limitation.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Quote the quartiles in (c) — an evaluation without figures is just an opinion."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 30. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''spread of the middle half'', 1 for the contrast with the median. (c) 3 marks: 1 for accepting the median value, 1 for citing 28–58, 1 for judging the claim''s limitation."},{"n":42,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two classes sit the same test out of 40.\n\nClass X: mean 26, median 27, range 22, standard deviation 5.2\nClass Y: mean 26, median 25, range 24, standard deviation 9.4\n\n(a) State which class has the more consistent results and justify it.\n(b) Explain what the medians suggest about the shape of each distribution.\n(c) A parent concludes ''the classes performed equally because both means are 26''. Evaluate this conclusion.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two classes sit the same test out of 40."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Class X: mean 26, median 27, range 22, standard deviation 5.2\nClass Y: mean 26, median 25, range 24, standard deviation 9.4"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State which class has the more consistent results and justify it.\n(b) Explain what the medians suggest about the shape of each distribution.\n(c) A parent concludes ''the classes performed equally because both means are 26''. Evaluate this conclusion."}]}]},"e":"σ tells us Class X is far more consistent. Median > mean suggests a low tail (X); median < mean suggests a high tail (Y). Equal means therefore mask very different distributions.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"comparing data sets","st":"comparing data sets","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":42,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Never judge two distributions on one measure. Centre, spread and shape each answer a different question.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State which class is more consistent and justify it.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the two standard deviations.","Which one is smaller?"],"strand":"D(i)","accepted":["class x, because its standard deviation of 5.2 is smaller than class y''s 9.4, so its marks are closer together"],"keywords":[["class"],["standard"],["deviation"],["smaller"],["marks"],["closer"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain what the medians suggest about each distribution''s shape.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Compare the median with the mean in each class.","If the median is below the mean, where is the tail?"],"strand":"D(ii)","accepted":["class x has median above mean, suggesting a low tail; class y has median below mean, suggesting a high tail pulling the mean up"],"keywords":[["class"],["median"],["above"],["mean"],["suggesting"],["tail"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate the parent''s conclusion.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Do equal means imply equal performance?","Use the spreads and medians in your answer."],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the means are equal but the distributions differ: class y is far more variable and its median is lower, so equal means do not mean equal performance"],"keywords":[["means"],["equal"],["distributions"],["differ"],["class"],["variable"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for naming X, 1 for citing σ. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison method, 1 for X''s low tail, 1 for Y''s high tail. (c) 3 marks: 1 for rejecting the conclusion, 1 for the spread evidence, 1 for a classroom consequence.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for naming X, 1 for citing σ. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison method, 1 for X''s low tail, 1 for Y''s high tail. (c) 3 marks: 1 for rejecting the conclusion, 1 for the spread evidence, 1 for a classroom consequence.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Compare centre, spread and skew separately in your answers; each is a distinct piece of evidence."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for naming X, 1 for citing σ. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison method, 1 for X''s low tail, 1 for Y''s high tail. (c) 3 marks: 1 for rejecting the conclusion, 1 for the spread evidence, 1 for a classroom consequence."},{"n":43,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A stem-and-leaf diagram of 20 masses, in grams, begins:\n\n2 | 1 4\n3 | 0 2 2 5 8\n4 | 1 4 6 9\n5 | 0 3\nKey: 3 | 2 means 32 g\n\n(a) State the smallest mass.\n(b) State how many masses are in the 40s.\n(c) State the mode.\n(d) A student says the median is 45. Without recalculating, explain why this cannot be correct.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stem-and-leaf diagram of 20 masses, in grams, begins:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2 | 1 4\n3 | 0 2 2 5 8\n4 | 1 4 6 9\n5 | 0 3\nKey: 3 | 2 means 32 g"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the smallest mass.\n(b) State how many masses are in the 40s.\n(c) State the mode.\n(d) A student says the median is 45. Without recalculating, explain why this cannot be correct."}]}]},"e":"Smallest 21; four values in the 40s; mode 32. For n = 20 the median is the average of the 10th and 11th values, so it is not simply read off one leaf, and 45 does not correspond to the leaves at those positions.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"stem-and-leaf diagrams","st":"stem-and-leaf diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":43,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Locate the median by position (n/2), not by eye. For an even n the median need not be a data value.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the smallest mass, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Read the key: the stem gives the tens.","The first leaf in the diagram is on stem 2."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":21,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State how many masses are in the 40s.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Every leaf on stem 4 is a mass in the forties.","Count the leaves 1, 4, 6 and 9."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the mode, in grams.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Which leaf value is repeated?","Look at stem 3."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":32,"tolerance":0,"unit":"g"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why a median of 45 g cannot be correct.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["With 20 values, the median is the average of the 10th and 11th.","Which values actually occupy those positions?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["with 20 values the median is the mean of the 10th and 11th values, which lie in the 40s row, but 45 is not a value in the diagram and a median need not be a data value, so 45 cannot be checked off the leaves"],"keywords":[["values"],["median"],["mean"],["10th"],["11th"],["value"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the position rule, 1 for locating the 10th and 11th, 1 for concluding why 45 fails.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the position rule, 1 for locating the 10th and 11th, 1 for concluding why 45 fails.","response_tools":["data_table","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"data_table","response_guidance":"Rebuild the stem-and-leaf diagram in the workspace and count positions — the position of the median is the argument."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the position rule, 1 for locating the 10th and 11th, 1 for concluding why 45 fails."},{"n":44,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A line graph shows a town''s recycling rate (%) each year: 2018: 12, 2019: 18, 2020: 24, 2021: 23, 2022: 29, 2023: 35.\n\n(a) Calculate the change in the recycling rate from 2018 to 2023.\n(b) State the year in which the rate fell, and by how many percentage points.\n(c) Calculate the mean yearly increase from 2018 to 2023, and explain why it may not predict 2024 accurately.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A line graph shows a town''s recycling rate (%) each year: 2018: 12, 2019: 18, 2020: 24, 2021: 23, 2022: 29, 2023: 35."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the change in the recycling rate from 2018 to 2023.\n(b) State the year in which the rate fell, and by how many percentage points.\n(c) Calculate the mean yearly increase from 2018 to 2023, and explain why it may not predict 2024 accurately."}]}]},"e":"12 → 35 is +23 points. The 2020→2021 change was a 1-point fall. The mean yearly increase over five intervals is 4.6 points, but extrapolating is unsound: growth is uneven and may saturate.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"line graphs","st":"line graphs","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":44,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An average rate of change smooths over the actual path; before extrapolating, check for reversals and ceilings.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the increase from 2018 to 2023, in percentage points.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Subtract the 2018 value from the 2023 value.","35 − 12."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":23,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the size of the fall in the year it decreased, in percentage points.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Compare consecutive years.","2020 was 24 and 2021 was 23."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the mean yearly increase from 2018 to 2023.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["There are five yearly intervals between 2018 and 2023.","Divide 23 by 5."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":4.6,"tolerance":0.05},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why this mean increase may not predict 2024 accurately.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Is the trend perfectly steady?","What happens to a percentage rate as it approaches its maximum?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the trend is not perfectly steady because one year fell, and growth is likely to slow as the rate approaches a practical maximum, so a simple average overstates future rises"],"keywords":[["trend"],["perfectly"],["steady"],["year"],["fell"],["growth"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the number of intervals, 1 for 4.6. (d) 3 marks: 1 for noting the fall, 1 for a ceiling argument, 1 for the conclusion about prediction.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the number of intervals, 1 for 4.6. (d) 3 marks: 1 for noting the fall, 1 for a ceiling argument, 1 for the conclusion about prediction.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the six points in the workspace and join them, then read the trend before answering (d)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the number of intervals, 1 for 4.6. (d) 3 marks: 1 for noting the fall, 1 for a ceiling argument, 1 for the conclusion about prediction."},{"n":45,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A researcher collects two data sets about the same 50 students.\n\nSet P: the score on a 100-mark test.\nSet Q: the time taken to complete the test, in minutes.\n\nThe researcher writes: ''Both data sets are quantitative, so both should be shown in a histogram with the same class widths.''\n\n(a) Explain what is correct in the statement.\n(b) Explain what is incorrect in the statement.\n(c) State the class width measured in marks that would be unsuitable for the time data, and explain why.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A researcher collects two data sets about the same 50 students."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set P: the score on a 100-mark test.\nSet Q: the time taken to complete the test, in minutes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The researcher writes: ''Both data sets are quantitative, so both should be shown in a histogram with the same class widths.''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Explain what is correct in the statement.\n(b) Explain what is incorrect in the statement.\n(c) State the class width measured in marks that would be unsuitable for the time data, and explain why."}]}]},"e":"Both variables are quantitative, so histograms are appropriate. But class widths must be chosen per variable: 10 marks suits a 0–100 range, while 10 minutes is too coarse for a typically 20–70 minute range, where 5-minute classes reveal the shape.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"classification of data","st":"classification of data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":45,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"B(i)","next_time_hint":"Choose class widths from the range and the unit of the variable, not from a template.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Explain what is correct in the researcher''s statement.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Are both variables numerical?","Can numerical data be grouped into classes?"],"strand":"B(i)","accepted":["both variables are quantitative, so a histogram is a suitable representation for each"],"keywords":[["variables"],["quantitative"],["histogram"],["suitable"],["representation"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain what is incorrect in the statement.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Do marks and minutes have the same range?","Should the class width depend on the variable?"],"strand":"B(ii)","accepted":["the class widths must suit each variable''s range and units; using the same widths for both misrepresents the time data"],"keywords":[["class"],["widths"],["suit"],["variable''s"],["range"],["units"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State a class width in marks that would be unsuitable for the time data, and explain why.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["A width of 10 is reasonable for marks out of 100.","How many classes would that leave for a time range of about 50 minutes?"],"strand":"B(iii)","accepted":["10 marks: for a time range of about 50 minutes it would create only about five classes, losing detail that a 5-minute width would show"],"keywords":[["marks"],["time"],["range"],["minutes"],["create"],["five"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''widths must suit the data'', 1 for the consequence. (c) 3 marks: 1 for naming a width, 1 for the range argument, 1 for the information lost.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''widths must suit the data'', 1 for the consequence. (c) 3 marks: 1 for naming a width, 1 for the range argument, 1 for the information lost.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"You may build both histograms in the workspace to see the effect of the class width.","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for ''widths must suit the data'', 1 for the consequence. (c) 3 marks: 1 for naming a width, 1 for the range argument, 1 for the information lost."}]'::jsonb) as x
